1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,020
Elliot, thanks for joining us today.

2
00:00:02,120 --> 00:00:06,000
So we have Elliot Prakta with us, and it's an honor to have you here spending some time with us

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:10,840
talking about the Elliot Wave principle and your history and your personal background on that.

4
00:00:11,300 --> 00:00:15,560
So, I mean, before we go into the more depth of Elliot Wave,

5
00:00:15,700 --> 00:00:18,800
if someone would be new to that kind of analysis,

6
00:00:19,420 --> 00:00:22,340
how would you describe Elliot Wave in simple sentences?

7
00:00:23,480 --> 00:00:26,360
Well, okay, I think the simplest way to understand it

8
00:00:26,360 --> 00:00:34,000
is it's a description of the price patterns that unfold over and over again in financial markets.

9
00:00:34,540 --> 00:00:37,680
So Elliot Wave theory is really the study of these price patterns.

10
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:42,160
So you could imagine that understanding the way these patterns behave

11
00:00:42,160 --> 00:00:45,400
might be of use to you if you're a trader or an investor.

12
00:00:46,020 --> 00:00:52,460
If we go back to the early 1930s when Elliot Wave theory was developed,

13
00:00:53,060 --> 00:00:55,120
can you give us a bit of the backdrop?

14
00:00:55,120 --> 00:00:58,480
How did the connection happen between the original...

15
00:00:58,480 --> 00:01:00,080
Now, let me rephrase this again. Sorry.

16
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:06,080
Let's look back to the early 1930s when the Elliot Wave theory was developed for his time.

17
00:01:06,180 --> 00:01:10,360
How did the connection happen between your father and the person that originally developed it?

18
00:01:11,740 --> 00:01:17,000
Well, the person who originally developed Elliot Wave theory was R.N Elliot, Ralph Nelson Elliot,

19
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:18,500
and he was an accountant.

20
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:24,140
And he actually became ill and was bedridden for a long period of time.

21
00:01:24,140 --> 00:01:30,800
And during that time, he spent hours and hours every day pouring over market charts,

22
00:01:31,060 --> 00:01:35,700
and he kept noticing these recurring patterns in price and time.

23
00:01:36,060 --> 00:01:40,640
And those patterns eventually became the Elliot Wave principle, which he later wrote about.

24
00:01:40,640 --> 00:01:46,720
And he actually used the theory to pinpoint the moment at which the Great Depression,

25
00:01:46,920 --> 00:01:49,540
the Great Crash of 29, ended.

26
00:01:49,940 --> 00:01:53,020
And he started telling people that they should be buying the market at that time.

27
00:01:53,200 --> 00:01:57,740
So he put it into practice quite readily from that point.

28
00:01:57,740 --> 00:02:05,660
My father, when he came across the Elliot Wave theory, believe it or not, the only way at that time,

29
00:02:05,800 --> 00:02:12,300
which was, I believe that was in the 70s, to read about the theory was on microfilm,

30
00:02:12,700 --> 00:02:14,720
because they didn't even have full books.

31
00:02:14,860 --> 00:02:20,040
So the information about it that R.N Elliot had written about was almost lost to history.

32
00:02:20,040 --> 00:02:28,220
And, of course, he had prints created from the microfilm and, of course, popularized it a lot during the 80s.

33
00:02:28,700 --> 00:02:33,840
So we almost didn't recover this theory.

34
00:02:34,180 --> 00:02:37,020
When did Elliot Wave theory really blow up?

35
00:02:37,160 --> 00:02:39,820
I mean, everybody on Wall Street knows Elliot Wave theory.

36
00:02:40,660 --> 00:02:44,540
The heyday when it was most popular was in the 1980s.

37
00:02:44,540 --> 00:02:53,040
My father had predicted a repeat of the roaring 20s right around the late 70s and early 80s,

38
00:02:53,040 --> 00:02:58,520
which, of course, went against the consensus at that time, which was mostly doom and gloom,

39
00:02:58,620 --> 00:03:06,560
which is completely understandable when you have interest rates at 18 percent and just very scary, you know, runaway inflation.

40
00:03:06,560 --> 00:03:15,920
I think that was the worst, I think it's the worst inflationary period in U.S. history, aside from maybe the continental, I guess.

41
00:03:16,860 --> 00:03:24,580
So you talked about your father making that public as a book and then going into this period where it gets known everywhere.

42
00:03:25,420 --> 00:03:30,060
How do you feel now, a generation after that, taking that forward?

43
00:03:30,200 --> 00:03:34,360
Or how has your father convinced you that you should continue that road?

44
00:03:34,360 --> 00:03:36,860
How does your first name relate to the...

45
00:03:36,860 --> 00:03:39,220
Yes, my first name.

46
00:03:39,360 --> 00:03:42,560
Well, it was either going to be Elliot or it was going to be Fibbo.

47
00:03:44,060 --> 00:03:46,080
I'm really glad I got with Elliot.

48
00:03:46,680 --> 00:03:49,600
Maybe if we had a dog, we'd call it Fibbo, I guess.

49
00:03:50,840 --> 00:03:56,460
But, no, for me, my background is in computer science and engineering.

50
00:03:57,880 --> 00:04:04,220
I really, growing up, I was never interested in finance at all, as most kids aren't, right?

51
00:04:04,220 --> 00:04:05,940
If you're interested in many other things.

52
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:06,840
I tried with my daughter.

53
00:04:09,600 --> 00:04:12,480
But, of course, you know, you grow up.

54
00:04:12,600 --> 00:04:17,020
And then the thing that really caught my attention was the crash of 2008.

55
00:04:17,520 --> 00:04:24,760
And that was when it just struck me that financial analysis has a big impact.

56
00:04:24,760 --> 00:04:31,220
And for me, it was beyond just a portfolio or numbers or even thinking about my own investments.

57
00:04:31,220 --> 00:04:39,700
It was more of a visceral feeling of, wow, these cycles in the market, they affect people in a very dramatic way.

58
00:04:39,760 --> 00:04:47,360
And I could see it going to work and people would be all depressed sitting at their offices back in 2008.

59
00:04:47,360 --> 00:04:51,880
Meanwhile, I'm like, you know, I'm 4x short the banks right now.

60
00:04:52,500 --> 00:04:58,780
I'm loving this, but I'm not going to say anything to anybody about it, you know, who's unhappy with what's happening.

61
00:04:59,080 --> 00:05:01,400
So that was a big hook for me.

62
00:05:01,640 --> 00:05:03,620
Like, oh, this is useful.

63
00:05:04,900 --> 00:05:08,040
And how should traders use Elliott Wave Analysis in practice?

64
00:05:08,040 --> 00:05:14,680
Is it a forecasting tool, a risk management tool, or a way to map out probabilities?

65
00:05:16,020 --> 00:05:18,340
Well, I think you pretty much laid all three out.

66
00:05:18,740 --> 00:05:19,960
You did a great job.

67
00:05:20,200 --> 00:05:20,980
This is or this.

68
00:05:21,060 --> 00:05:23,260
I was like, yes to all three.

69
00:05:23,880 --> 00:05:27,740
You know, I mean, historically, it's mainly been a long-term forecasting tool.

70
00:05:28,260 --> 00:05:32,040
Personally, I like the intermediate time frame a little more.

71
00:05:32,140 --> 00:05:36,240
And for that reason is the second reason you mentioned, which is limiting your risk.

72
00:05:36,240 --> 00:05:42,860
Because everyone I know who is a successful trader, I'm sure there are exceptions out there.

73
00:05:42,960 --> 00:05:49,280
But the ones that I know personally, their number one thing is limiting the risk when they're wrong.

74
00:05:49,960 --> 00:05:58,040
And that's what Elliott Wave Theory does a really good job of doing, is it tells you precise points in time where, if you have a thesis,

75
00:05:58,580 --> 00:06:03,140
where that thesis will be validated and where that thesis will be invalidated.

76
00:06:03,140 --> 00:06:07,440
So you can try to keep your risk as small as possible.

77
00:06:07,760 --> 00:06:13,960
So when I started at Bear Stearns in the early 90s, I remember, you know, taking tickets, get them time-stemmed.

78
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:15,720
It was all still very basic.

79
00:06:16,180 --> 00:06:23,960
How did technology change the functionality of Elliott Wave?

80
00:06:23,960 --> 00:06:28,100
Or are there no big changes compared to the 70s or 80s?

81
00:06:28,620 --> 00:06:29,980
That's an interesting question.

82
00:06:30,640 --> 00:06:37,720
And when I talked today at the FSC, I mentioned at the end of my presentation a point about that exactly,

83
00:06:37,960 --> 00:06:45,960
which is that we found that the behavior of Elliott Waves really hasn't changed in spite of the technology.

84
00:06:45,960 --> 00:06:51,660
In fact, the term I use is it's a stationary model.

85
00:06:52,260 --> 00:07:01,040
And what I mean by stationary is that the behavior or the properties of these patterns don't really change through time.

86
00:07:02,300 --> 00:07:09,720
In fact, the behavior of Elliott Waves, as far as I can tell from my work, is the same today as it was 200 years ago.

87
00:07:09,720 --> 00:07:18,180
So we think markets, despite all the technology, there's still a human being behind that's calling the shots,

88
00:07:18,440 --> 00:07:22,940
even if you have, say, a fully quantitative model that's automated.

89
00:07:23,240 --> 00:07:26,500
At the end of the day, there's still a person who developed the model,

90
00:07:26,940 --> 00:07:31,900
a person who's still running or deciding when to stop the model if they don't like the results.

91
00:07:32,560 --> 00:07:37,540
So it's still, I think, humans are still the drivers of what happens in the market.

92
00:07:37,540 --> 00:07:40,560
And I think Elliott Waves are a reflection of that psychology.

93
00:07:41,240 --> 00:07:46,760
There's a great saying on Wall Street, if you follow the herd, all you see is asses.

94
00:07:48,520 --> 00:07:54,560
How do you avoid being part of the herd at important turning points in the market,

95
00:07:54,700 --> 00:07:56,840
looking at using an Elliott Wave theory?

96
00:07:57,440 --> 00:08:02,040
Well, I think what Elliott Waves do for you, and I would say technical analysis in general,

97
00:08:02,040 --> 00:08:12,680
Elliott Waves and cycles as well, is that they tune your mind to think about and anticipate change in the market.

98
00:08:13,340 --> 00:08:19,460
So instead of focusing on the fact that the market has been going up for a really long time

99
00:08:19,460 --> 00:08:22,860
and then, say, just extrapolating that infinitely into the future,

100
00:08:22,860 --> 00:08:30,360
you're always thinking that this trend really could turn at any moment and become a different kind of a trend.

101
00:08:30,980 --> 00:08:33,680
So it's just a different way of thinking.

102
00:08:34,460 --> 00:08:38,260
And when you're thinking that way, you're not going to be as caught off guard

103
00:08:38,260 --> 00:08:41,560
when there's a sudden change in the market environment,

104
00:08:41,560 --> 00:08:46,080
you know, like when bond yields go from zero or in some cases negative

105
00:08:46,080 --> 00:08:49,120
and suddenly the highest yields in 20 years,

106
00:08:49,480 --> 00:08:52,600
you know, those are the kinds of things that catch a lot of people off guard.

107
00:08:53,060 --> 00:08:58,660
But if you look at, for example, the wave counts that Elliott Wave International has been producing,

108
00:08:59,240 --> 00:09:03,740
you know, they anticipated that change in interest rates before it happened.

109
00:09:04,100 --> 00:09:06,520
So, you know, you're not always going to be right,

110
00:09:06,520 --> 00:09:12,760
but at least you will be in tune and ready for those junctures to occur.

111
00:09:13,420 --> 00:09:20,460
Are there differences between applying Elliott Wave theory on equities, bonds, currencies, cryptocurrencies?

112
00:09:22,140 --> 00:09:23,460
There are some differences.

113
00:09:24,360 --> 00:09:27,740
With the E-Wave software in particular that I developed,

114
00:09:28,080 --> 00:09:30,700
I treat all markets the same, actually.

115
00:09:31,040 --> 00:09:36,500
But there's certainly an argument to be made that some of them do trade a little bit differently.

116
00:09:36,520 --> 00:09:40,440
But I'm always wary of curve fitting.

117
00:09:40,840 --> 00:09:49,540
You know, I don't want to bifurcate the training data I use for the program to too many different buckets.

118
00:09:49,820 --> 00:09:54,140
You know, the more general that I can treat things, the more robust I think it is.

119
00:09:54,400 --> 00:09:55,800
But there are definitely differences.

120
00:09:56,180 --> 00:10:01,220
And one example that I gave earlier today was that in commodity markets,

121
00:10:01,220 --> 00:10:08,500
you tend to get these large fifth-wave blow-off moves more often than you get in, say, equities.

122
00:10:08,700 --> 00:10:11,280
So we do know that there are some differences.

123
00:10:13,280 --> 00:10:15,740
Let's also get back to the technology topic.

124
00:10:15,860 --> 00:10:18,420
And you mentioned E-Wave already a little bit in your mentioning.

125
00:10:18,540 --> 00:10:22,460
Because I think, I mean, you mentioned we go from microfilm to books.

126
00:10:22,560 --> 00:10:24,260
This is what your father led the way.

127
00:10:24,260 --> 00:10:29,080
But I think now you add another layer on top of that, because you mentioned E-Wave technologies.

128
00:10:29,860 --> 00:10:34,780
So these days we have the computer power to detect patterns, as you introduced.

129
00:10:34,940 --> 00:10:36,960
Elliot waves are patterns in price structure.

130
00:10:37,720 --> 00:10:40,400
So, and I think a challenge always was with Elliot waves.

131
00:10:40,600 --> 00:10:47,440
Someone needs to sit in front of a price chart and make a decision where to put this pattern.

132
00:10:47,440 --> 00:10:51,080
And it's always easy to see a pattern after the fact.

133
00:10:52,180 --> 00:10:56,940
But the challenge is to see it quasi before it happens or before it is completed.

134
00:10:57,240 --> 00:10:59,820
I mean, that's where we want to use Elliot wave for.

135
00:11:00,240 --> 00:11:05,620
And I think you are now pushing this into a kind of computer-automated, supported way,

136
00:11:05,940 --> 00:11:09,060
which is, I think, another big step which is required.

137
00:11:09,180 --> 00:11:14,020
Because every Elliot wave analyst might come up with a different count.

138
00:11:14,020 --> 00:11:16,420
So how do you see that?

139
00:11:16,640 --> 00:11:20,000
And what is E-Wave maybe solving in that context?

140
00:11:21,140 --> 00:11:25,520
Yes, well, that's a lot to unpack there all in one question.

141
00:11:25,900 --> 00:11:30,340
So I'm not sure which part of the question to answer first here.

142
00:11:31,820 --> 00:11:33,600
Okay, where do we start?

143
00:11:35,180 --> 00:11:38,720
Maybe I'll start with creating E-Waves.

144
00:11:38,720 --> 00:11:44,480
We could start in the beginning here before we get on to the other part of that.

145
00:11:45,340 --> 00:11:48,700
But E-Waves is a project that actually started a long time ago.

146
00:11:48,880 --> 00:11:50,020
It started in 1986.

147
00:11:50,700 --> 00:11:53,680
So it's been in development for a while.

148
00:11:54,200 --> 00:11:57,060
I say that, but that's not entirely true.

149
00:11:57,460 --> 00:12:00,240
The version 1 was created in 86.

150
00:12:00,240 --> 00:12:08,120
And then it more or less was not really used for a long time or developed on.

151
00:12:08,500 --> 00:12:16,180
And the reason, if you used computers back in the 80s, you might have some idea that they didn't have a lot of horsepower behind them.

152
00:12:16,660 --> 00:12:23,580
And it actually takes a tremendous amount of computing power to do these Elliot wave analysis.

153
00:12:23,580 --> 00:12:32,920
Even today, and I've got a really souped-up computer that I use for it, and even then it's a lot slower than I would like.

154
00:12:33,040 --> 00:12:36,720
So there's just, there's a lot of computing power that's necessary.

155
00:12:37,800 --> 00:12:46,700
So I would say it wasn't until more recently that we really had the power we needed to be able to run the program well.

156
00:12:46,700 --> 00:12:56,900
And, of course, what I've been doing is I rewrote the entire thing and modernized it, you know, and kind of brought it up into the modern era, so to speak.

157
00:12:57,860 --> 00:13:01,540
And so that's the E-Waves origin story.

158
00:13:01,880 --> 00:13:09,540
But I think you have included a way to find, isolate, and detect counts and wave patterns right now.

159
00:13:09,840 --> 00:13:11,880
So you don't need to do it manually.

160
00:13:12,160 --> 00:13:14,240
So E-Wave is doing that for you, right?

161
00:13:14,240 --> 00:13:18,340
Yes. I mean, E-Waves does do the wave count.

162
00:13:18,460 --> 00:13:24,380
It's an automated process, so you don't have to sit there by hand and sketch out the wave pattern,

163
00:13:25,060 --> 00:13:29,480
which, like you said earlier, can be very tricky because when you're looking at a chart,

164
00:13:30,740 --> 00:13:33,300
you're going to have partial patterns most of the time.

165
00:13:33,820 --> 00:13:41,220
And that's why, in my presentation earlier, I wanted to show that particular example where we had the market coiling up

166
00:13:41,220 --> 00:13:45,820
and it looked like it was a triangle, and we're going to burst up out of the triangle, right,

167
00:13:45,880 --> 00:13:52,660
which is a common pattern, and the market did the exact opposite because that was actually part of a partial pattern.

168
00:13:52,780 --> 00:13:54,060
It was not a finished triangle.

169
00:13:54,200 --> 00:13:55,980
It was part of yet another pattern.

170
00:13:55,980 --> 00:14:00,680
So there's all these kind of very tricky situations you get into with partial patterns.

171
00:14:01,140 --> 00:14:11,820
So it's been quite a challenge to develop the program to deal with the inherent partialness of the data we're working with,

172
00:14:11,820 --> 00:14:21,180
but that's why the program does a lot of extrapolation work, like I showed, where it can try to complete the pattern in its head.

173
00:14:21,260 --> 00:14:28,640
So it's always kind of imagining, okay, if this is a partially formed pattern, where might the market go next and what would that look like?

174
00:14:29,180 --> 00:14:34,520
Does it remove the subjective decision-making of the analyst, which we had before,

175
00:14:34,520 --> 00:14:40,940
which made me crazy following different Elliott Wave experts showing different counts?

176
00:14:41,060 --> 00:14:42,400
I think that cannot be the case.

177
00:14:42,500 --> 00:14:45,080
There are clear rules published in the book.

178
00:14:45,320 --> 00:14:47,760
So why can they come up with different outcomes?

179
00:14:48,340 --> 00:14:52,440
So does E-Wave now solve that issue or not?

180
00:14:53,160 --> 00:14:53,460
Yeah.

181
00:14:53,860 --> 00:14:59,960
Okay, so there are, of course, rules that do constrain the wave counts that are legal,

182
00:15:00,260 --> 00:15:03,180
as well as the wave counts that are illegal, so to speak.

183
00:15:03,180 --> 00:15:07,160
And that's one common baseline that all analysts should follow.

184
00:15:07,520 --> 00:15:11,500
I've seen many blog posts where they obviously don't know about the rules,

185
00:15:11,620 --> 00:15:13,580
and you get some really weird-looking counts.

186
00:15:14,320 --> 00:15:21,180
But even at that basis, okay, that's a nice foundation for the Elliott Wave theory.

187
00:15:21,580 --> 00:15:27,680
But then there's, of course, the area where probability comes into play and guidelines come into play.

188
00:15:27,680 --> 00:15:33,960
And when you get to that area of the theory, where you have different alternative wave counts

189
00:15:33,960 --> 00:15:38,440
or different ways that are legal of labeling a chart and you're trying to pick between them,

190
00:15:38,760 --> 00:15:41,820
that's where you get different analysts that may disagree.

191
00:15:42,620 --> 00:15:47,240
Now, I think there are times in the market where everything kind of lines up,

192
00:15:47,320 --> 00:15:51,540
and you'll see many analysts all of a sudden agreeing, and I love when that happens.

193
00:15:51,540 --> 00:15:58,040
So my answer to you is when you see analysts disagreeing, as long as they're good analysts,

194
00:15:58,140 --> 00:16:05,180
what it's telling you is that there are some alternative interpretations with a different message that are going on.

195
00:16:05,240 --> 00:16:11,900
So maybe the best thing to do is to just do this for a little bit and say, okay, the message is not clear.

196
00:16:12,160 --> 00:16:14,880
Very similar to the cycles work you were talking about,

197
00:16:14,960 --> 00:16:18,320
where you say sometimes there are cycles going in different directions,

198
00:16:18,320 --> 00:16:20,140
and the right answer is to be neutral.

199
00:16:20,660 --> 00:16:23,220
So I would say it's the same with Elliott Waves.

200
00:16:23,960 --> 00:16:30,440
So what we've done with E-Waves, of course, that a human can't do is that instead of using guidelines,

201
00:16:30,780 --> 00:16:33,940
you know, I use the word guidelines because that's what we're all familiar with,

202
00:16:34,420 --> 00:16:37,720
but E-Waves, the program itself does not have guidelines.

203
00:16:38,100 --> 00:16:44,160
You know, the guidelines, so to speak, that I refer to are just part of what the model,

204
00:16:44,160 --> 00:16:51,600
how the model learns the behavior of the patterns, which is an automatic learning process that it does.

205
00:16:52,800 --> 00:16:57,020
But we can, of course, study the resulting model and kind of see, hey, does it,

206
00:16:57,260 --> 00:17:02,720
is it behaving in a way that conforms to the original guidelines that Aaron Elliott wrote about?

207
00:17:02,720 --> 00:17:09,940
And like I said before, we've, for the most part, have found that it's in agreement with his observations.

208
00:17:09,940 --> 00:17:14,520
Is there any cross-checking taking place between the results of E-Waves

209
00:17:14,520 --> 00:17:19,960
and the official Elliott Wave International interpretation of what the markets are doing?

210
00:17:20,800 --> 00:17:21,860
Cross-checking?

211
00:17:22,060 --> 00:17:25,560
Yeah, that you compare and you see if there are differences.

212
00:17:25,560 --> 00:17:32,960
You know, to be honest, we've tried to have various analysts follow the same course.

213
00:17:33,540 --> 00:17:38,080
But if you've ever worked with analysts before, you know that they each have their own opinion.

214
00:17:38,860 --> 00:17:43,620
And so people do much better work when they're allowed to just use their own opinion.

215
00:17:43,920 --> 00:17:47,260
